/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  batch13_nacl_pitzer  --  batch crystalliser whose saturation comes from ION
                           ACTIVITY, not from a solubility curve.

  The sibling of batch05 (sugar), and deliberately so: same population balance,
  same method of moments, same RK4 -- and a different route to the ONE number
  the whole model turns on, the supersaturation S.

  batch05 asks a molecular solubility curve for c_sat.  This case declares a
  Pitzer package, so the saturated molality is the one that satisfies
  Ksp = (gamma_pm * m_sat)^2 -- the ion activity product, solved for m_sat by
  the SAME resolver the steady crystalliser uses.  A student can put the two
  cases side by side and see that "supersaturation" is not one recipe: it is
  whatever the declared thermodynamics says saturation is.

  Until 2026-07-31 this case could not have been written honestly: the batch
  model computed c/c_sat off the molecular curve and had no way to reach the
  ionic route, which lived in an anonymous namespace inside the steady unit.
  It would have resolved ions for its activity coefficients AND its heat of
  crystallisation, then taken S from a curve -- silently.

  A supersaturated brine (m0 ~ 7.6 mol/kg against m_sat ~ 6.14 at 25 C, the
  state a post-evaporation liquor arrives in) relaxes: crystals nucleate and
  grow, the dissolved salt depletes, S decays toward 1.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

application   choupoBatch;
description   "Batch NaCl crystalliser: saturation from the Pitzer ion activity, not a solubility curve";

verbosity     3;

startTime       0;        // s
endTime         7200;     // s   (2 h)
deltaT          2.0;      // s   (RK4 step)
writeInterval   360;      // s
